\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

We derive a lightweight online detector monitoring the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth (Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}). Applied to 14 runs across three environments, it achieves TPR${=}5/5$ (95\% Clopper-Pearson CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$), FPR${=}2/9$ ($22\%$), with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases (Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). The detector requires larger-sample validation before deployment as a practical diagnostic tool.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Online CIVO Detector. Monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio $r_t = \bar{V}_t / \bar{E}_t$ (not NVE) for superlinear growth indicative of CIVO onset.}
\label{alg:civo_detector}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Stream of $(\bar{V}_t, \bar{E}_t)$ at each checkpoint $t$
\STATE $\tau_\text{mag} \leftarrow 0.49$, \quad $\tau_\text{vel} \leftarrow 0.1$, \quad $\varepsilon \leftarrow 10^{-6}$
\STATE Initialize $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow 0$, $\mathcal{H} \leftarrow []$
\FOR{each checkpoint $t$}
  \STATE $r_t \leftarrow \bar{V}_t \,/\, \max(\bar{E}_t,\, \varepsilon)$
  \STATE $\Delta r_t \leftarrow r_t - r_\text{prev}$
  \STATE $c_\text{mag} \leftarrow (r_t \geq \tau_\text{mag})$
  \STATE $c_\text{vel} \leftarrow (\Delta r_t > \tau_\text{vel})$
  \STATE $c_\text{acc} \leftarrow (\Delta r_\text{prev} > \tau_\text{vel}) \wedge (\Delta r_t > \Delta r_\text{prev})$
  \STATE $c_\text{dec} \leftarrow \bigl|\{i : \mathcal{H}[i] < \mathcal{H}[i{-}1],\; i \in \text{last 3}\}\bigr| \geq 2$
  \IF{$c_\text{mag} \wedge c_\text{vel} \wedge c_\text{acc} \wedge c_\text{dec}$}
    \STATE \textbf{Alert:} CIVO onset at step $t$
  \ENDIF
  \STATE $r_\text{prev} \leftarrow r_t$, \quad $\Delta r_\text{prev} \leftarrow \Delta r_t$
  \STATE Append $\bar{E}_t$ to $\mathcal{H}$
\ENDFOR
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

World-model reinforcement learning enables sample-efficient learning directly on physical hardware \citep{wu2023daydreamer, hansen2024tdmpc2}. Real robotic tasks demand temporal abstraction: manipulation requires fast reactive grasping alongside slow approach planning, locomotion involves gait phases at different frequencies, and sim-to-real transfer must handle $\dt$ mismatches between simulation and hardware. TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?

We find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration degrades performance catastrophically (14--19\% of baseline). Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \emph{trained} $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the strongest predictor. This combination enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78$--$3.11\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates, a failure mode we term \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO), analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced. A normalized value-to-eval (NVE) threshold of $\geq 1.5\times$ achieves 53/56 classification ($n{=}56$, 24 CIVO-positive, 32 healthy; AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sensitivity}).
Gradient decomposition reveals that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes 38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$ yet reward-only stopping fails, while the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal. A four-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation, architecture) shows that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments. In pure-imagination training, dual critics converge to identical value functions ($\rho > 0.999$), rendering Double Q pessimism ineffective; the min operation nonetheless enables CIVO recovery, while prevention requires min $+$ increased capacity. These findings are directly relevant to robotics: as world-model RL moves toward real systems with variable-rate sensing and multi-timescale control, understanding when temporal conditioning fails, and why standard countermeasures cannot fix it, is essential for safe deployment.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Failure mode identification.} We identify CIVO as specific to imagination-based actor-critic with temporal conditioning and isolate trained $\dt$-embedding $+$ multi-rate exposure as jointly sufficient in tested configurations (7/7 multi-joint locomotion seeds, 0/3 single-joint, 0/3 manipulation; AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$). Cross-environment validation spanning locomotion, manipulation (ManiSkill), and a single-joint control task (Cartpole) establishes that CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion; frozen-embedding controls on three manipulation tasks and the Cartpole negative control (0/3 seeds, VE ratio $< 0.1$) identify value-ratio transients as environment-driven, demonstrating the diagnostic's specificity on non-CIVO dynamics.
    \item \textbf{Architectural boundary.} $\dt$-conditioning succeeds in TD-MPC2's MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping, where model errors compound into biased value targets with no real-data anchor.
    \item \textbf{Four-level intervention hierarchy.} Across gradient, output, representation, and architecture levels, no intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments. Gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance (reward head: 38--49\%, reward-only stopping fails). In pure-imagination training, dual critics converge ($\rho > 0.999$), eliminating Double Q pessimism, though the min operation enables recovery through a different mechanism.
    \item \textbf{Preliminary diagnostic.} A proof-of-concept online detector (distinct from the NVE threshold classifier) achieves TPR 95\% CI $[47.8\%, 100\%]$ ($5/5$ positives), FPR $22\%$ ($2/9$), with predictive warning in 2/5 cases ($n{=}14$ runs, three environments; \S\ref{sec:detector}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM develops a $2.78$--$3.11\times$ reward prediction gap under trained conditioning (reward MAE $0.057 \to 0.178$ at $\dt{=}2$; Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), while observation reconstruction actually \emph{improves} (gap $1.33\times$ vs.\ baseline $1.46\times$), confirming a reward-channel-specific pathology analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional}. Gradient magnitude $\neq$ causal importance: the reward head (38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$) fails when blocked alone; the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical signal; full-pathway SGS (0/3) confirms forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability. CIVO is upstream of estimation error---dual critics converge (\S\ref{sec:arch_results})---so standard corrections are inapplicable; MPC re-planning prevents accumulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). Time-series analysis across 12 dense checkpoints resolves a three-stage causal chain ($n{=}2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}): reward fidelity degradation (SS ratio $>1.1\times$ at 20K) precedes value overestimation onset (${\sim}$30K) by ${\sim}$10K steps ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$), which in turn precedes actor entropy explosion ${\sim}35$K steps later ($+2.1$ nats/10K in collapsed configs vs.\ $+0.07$--$0.22$ in healthy baselines; Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}). However, entropy is a lagging indicator only: at matched time points during the CIVO onset window, collapsed and healthy runs are indistinguishable ($\Delta = 0.03$ nats at 55K; Appendix~\ref{sec:entropy_not_diagnostic}). This three-stage chain---fidelity $\to$ value $\to$ (late) entropy---confirms that the collapse mechanism is critic-side; the actor remains confident but wrong until well after value divergence.

\paragraph{Causal role of embedding training.}
The three-way ablation (trained/frozen/removed $\dt$-embedding; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}) disambiguates two hypotheses: CIVO could arise from the architectural presence of a $\dt$-conditioning pathway, or specifically from learned $\dt$-specific representations shaped by gradient flow. Since frozen embeddings participate in the forward pass identically to trained ones yet do not trigger CIVO (NVE $0.40\times$ vs.\ $6.57\times$), the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}---near-antipodal representations (\S\ref{sec:cosine_evolution}; temporal dynamics in \S\ref{sec:cosine_temporal}) that amplify reward prediction gaps---rather than in conditioning architecture per se. For world-model RL systems using DreamerV3's RSSM, this suggests that auxiliary conditioning signals whose gradient flow touches the dynamics model require fidelity guarantees matching the model's prediction accuracy; without such guarantees, learned conditioning creates exploitable structure that imagination-based critics cannot self-correct.

\paragraph{Intervention resistance.}
Seven targeted interventions across four levels fail to reliably eliminate CIVO (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}): gradient stopping shifts co-adaptation to unblocked pathways---obs-decoder-only SGS on Cheetah \emph{amplifies} NVE from $8.3\times$ to $21.9$--$30.2\times$ (compensatory amplification; blocking one pathway intensifies co-adaptation through the remainder). Output capping (RAVE) is defeated by its own EMA anchor tracking biased values. Representation noise (TCN) exhibits an impossible-dose dilemma: the effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$ on Cheetah) suppresses CIVO entirely (NVE${\leq}0.3\times$) but erodes policy to $15\%$ of baseline through cumulative actor gradient interference, while lower doses ($\sigma{=}0.2$) succeed on only 2/4 Walker seeds and 0/3 cross-environment. The pattern indicates pathway redundancy---CIVO exploits whichever gradient channel remains open, and partial interventions can worsen severity---consistent with it being a systemic property of imagination-based training with temporal conditioning rather than a defect in any single component.

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
Monitor via NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$ at $n{=}56$ across two environments; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}). Freezing the $\dt$-embedding converts CIVO onset (7/7 trained locomotion, NVE $1.53$--$108\times$) to transient spikes that self-correct within 20K steps (2/3 frozen Walker, $1.71$--$1.88\times$; 0/2 frozen Cheetah); untreated, extreme CIVO ($70.6\times$) causes irrecoverable damage (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}).

\paragraph{Training horizon.}
Our primary analysis uses 105K steps, sufficient to observe CIVO onset (45K--65K) and classify trajectories. Extended training (${\sim}$302K steps, one seed) shows complete self-recovery after approximately 170K additional steps post-onset---at substantial computational cost that the frozen $\dt$-embedding diagnostic eliminates entirely (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_training}).

\paragraph{Implications for robot learning.}
No $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO was observed in manipulation (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}). Both domains use dense shaped rewards, so reward sparsity cannot explain the boundary. A preliminary hypothesis is that locomotion's periodic state-action dynamics create stronger dependence on RSSM temporal prediction accuracy---small $\dt$-conditioned reward biases compound over cyclical gaits---whereas manipulation's contact-gated transitions are less sensitive to temporal conditioning fidelity. Other possible mediators include action-space periodicity and observation structure; the precise factors remain an open question.

