Entropy descent rate correlates with temporal configuration complexity, not collapse fate. Simpler configurations (baseline $\dt{=}1$, unexposed exp\_008a) reach $-4.7$ by 25K, while configurations exposed to temporal variation (both collapsed and healthy) remain near $-2.0$ at 25K, reflecting the difficulty of policy commitment when training data spans multiple temporal scales.

\paragraph{Long-horizon entropy recovery.}
The baseline ($\dt{=}1$) run extended to 505K steps shows entropy recovering from $-5.2$ to ${\sim}{-}1.5$ between 115K and 295K steps, coinciding with continued performance improvement ($700 \to 950$ return). This adaptive entropy increase rules out entropy rise as an inherently negative signal; the actor maintains ${\sim}950$ return with entropy 3--4 nats above its minimum, indicating a healthy exploration--exploitation balance during extended training.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The two collapse patterns identified in \S\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and a tentative second mode (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures. Pathway~B is observed in only two seeds (008e\_v3\_s2 and 038\_s2); we present it as preliminary evidence that the trained $\dt$-embedding's learned features may affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B), though additional data is needed to confirm this as a distinct mechanism.  This explains why $\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\times$ for 008e\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse (Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}).


\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}
\label{sec:implications}

CIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training.  Hierarchical approaches (THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine}) may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.  The CIVO mechanism requires three structural properties: (1) a temporal embedding that co-adapts with dynamics via gradient flow, (2) multi-step imagined rollouts used for critic training, and (3) a reward head that inherits latent biases from the shared representation.  Transformer-based world models that share these properties may exhibit analogous vulnerability; architectures that decouple temporal conditioning from the reward prediction pathway would not.

